   141	                     2. Complete presentation of MB1,3
   142	  To obtain a canonical form of a word in an algebra, the diamond lemma by
   143	G. Bergman [4] is extremely useful. To understand the notions of ambiguities and
   144	canonical words, we start with his terminology.
   145	Definition 2.1 ([4]). Let α1 = ut and α2 = tv be two words consisting of the
   146	left-hand sides of two relations Ri and Rj in M B1,3 . The word of the form utv is
   147	said to be an ambiguity and we denote it by Ri − Rj .
   148	   A word containing a sub-word of the left-hand side of any relation of a braid
   149	monoid is called a reducible word, and a word that does not contain any sub-word
   150	of the left-hand side of any relation is called an irreducible (or canonical) word.
   151	Definition 2.2 ([5]). Let G be a finitely generated group and S be a finite set of
   152	generators of G. The word length lS (g) of an element g ∈ G is the smallest integer
   153	n for which there exist s1 , . . . , sn ∈ S ∪ S −1 such that g = s1 · · · sn .
   154	   The diamond lemma says that a set of relations is complete if all the ambiguities
   155	are solved. We call a complete set of relations in MB1,3 a complete presentation of
   156	MB1,3 . The other names for the complete presentation are being used as Gröbner
   157	bases, presentation with solvable ambiguities and rewriting system, etc. We find
