    11	- Write `A(B_n)` for the Artin–Tits group of Coxeter type B, to avoid confusing it with the ordinary n-strand braid group `B_n=A(A_{n-1})`. Coxeter groups additionally impose `s_i^2=1`; a basis for that quotient is not a basis for the Artin group.
    12	- The book's **Artin–Garside alphabet is the small alphabet `{sigma_i, Delta, Delta^{-1}}`**. A presentation on **all nonidentity simple elements** (positive lifts of `W\{1}`) is a different alphabet, even when its authors call it “Garside's presentation”. Similarly, a dual-monoid presentation or a Koszul/PBW theorem is not automatically a completed GS basis in BKL/reflection generators.
    13	- A finite convergent rewriting presentation implies unique irreducible words on its own alphabet. It does not automatically supply a GS basis for a prescribed *different* alphabet or prescribed monomial order. A coherent presentation adds relations between relations; coherence alone is not confluence of the oriented standard relations.
    14	- GS bases can be infinite. In particular, “no finite basis for this degree-lex order” is not “no GS basis”. Mere abstract existence by including all equalities is not the explicit/effective problem posed in the book; compare Autord [AU07], §1.2.
