    35	| **(4) Finite-type Artin groups in BKL generators** | **partially solved** | **Type A**: Bokut [BKL09] gives a GS basis in BKL generators **enlarged by `delta^{±1}`**, with degree-lex `delta^{-1}<delta<a_ts` and lexicographic order on `(t,s)`. For other finite types, Bessis [BE03] constructs the dual Garside monoid and Picantin [PI02] supplies explicit type-B/D dual presentations; neither opened source asserts the needed completed GS basis in the small BKL/reflection alphabet. Josuat-Vergès–Nadeau [JN23] proves Koszulity of the dual monoid algebra and a PBW statement for its **quadratic dual**, not a group GS basis. The non-A generator-specific question remains unclosed in this search. |
    36	| **(5) Extend (1)–(3) to arbitrary Artin groups** | **partially solved** | Type A and several restricted group classes have strong results: [BV06], [CZ13], [HM99], Holt–Rees [HR12]. A major broader **monoid** result is Dehornoy–Guiraud [DG16], Corollary 6.3.4: every finitely generated Artin–Tits monoid has a **finite quadratic convergent presentation on a finite Garside-family alphabet**; finiteness comes from [DDH15]. Gaussent–Guiraud–Malbos [GGM15] gives a convergent presentation on positive lifts of `W\{1}` and a coherent standard Artin presentation. These are not bases for arbitrary **groups** in the prescribed Burau or atom-plus-Delta alphabets. No such general group result was found. |
    37	| **(6) Singular braid monoid SB_n in Artin–Garside and BKL generators** | **open as far as found** | Vershinin [VS10], opened PDF, proves Garside and greedy normal forms in the singular setting and gives a BKL-style presentation. Theorem 1 uses the usual positive braid/singular letters with the lex order `sigma1<...<sigma(n-1)<x1<...<x(n-1)` for its positive base; Theorem 6 uses `a_ts^{±1}, b_qp`. These are **normal-form/presentation results, not a displayed GS completion in both requested alphabets**. Chaynikov [CH06] is an important BKL normal-form lead already known to the book, but mathematical content was not opened here: **[UNVERIFIED]**. “Singular part of the Brauer semigroup” is a different object and was excluded. |
    38	| **(7) VB_n, WB_n, IB_n, and complex-reflection braid groups B(W), standard generators** | **open as far as found** | No reliable opened source closes this combined question. Karpuz–Özalan [KO20] claims GS bases for **pure** virtual `PV_n` on `mu_ij^{±1}`, not full `VB_n`; the displayed basis also has the inverse-overlap caveat in C. Karpuz–Özalan–Çevik [KOC19] claims a `B(G24)` GS basis on `s,t,u` and inverses, but has a similar displayed-list caveat. Çevik–Albargi [CA20] is a false positive: despite its title, its Theorem 3.1 imposes `s²=t²=u²=1` and works with **G12**, not a verified basis for `B(G12)`. Vershinin [VI09] gives inverse-braid normal forms/word-problem methods, not the specified GS basis. Çevik et al. [CI20] treats the **symmetric inverse monoid I_n**, not `IB_n`. For complex braid groups, [CLL15] and [FM26] provide presentations/Garside-type structures, not completed standard-generator GS bases. No direct welded GS theorem was located. |
    39	
    40	For (3) and (4), “partially solved” includes the pre-2009 type-A baseline because the printed question says “finite-type Artin groups”. If “the same types as (2)” is imposed, the **residual non-A status is “open as far as found”** in the specified alphabets. The table does not count changed-generator rewriting results as solutions of that residual problem.
