    44	### B1. Explicit GS results and counterexamples
    45	
    46	**[CL09] Yuqun Chen and Cihua Liu**, *Gröbner–Shirshov bases for Coxeter groups I*, arXiv preprint (2009), [arXiv:0910.0096](https://arxiv.org/abs/0910.0096). **Opened abstract and PDF.** Example 3.2 has `m12=m23=m24=infinity`, `m13=3`, `m14=2`, `m34=5`, with `s1<s2<s3<s4` and degree-lex. It disproves obtaining the conjectured basis using only commutations: the noncommuting length-three braid relation is also needed. The new conjecture concerns a pre-GS basis, not the original formula. Local text lines 184–219 are the critical passage. The general-case theorem excludes four types of nontrivial inclusion composition; do not turn it into a uniform solution.
    47	
    48	**[BS01] L. A. Bokut and L.-S. Shiao**, *Gröbner–Shirshov bases for Coxeter groups*, *Communications in Algebra* 29 (2001), 4305–4319, [DOI:10.1081/AGB-100106002](https://doi.org/10.1081/AGB-100106002). **[UNVERIFIED] original mathematical text.** DOI metadata opened but supplied no abstract; publisher text was not obtained. Its classical A/B/D results and conjecture are described by the opened [CL09], [PW26] and 2014 survey. The counterexample claim above relies on [CL09], not merely on this metadata.
    49	
    50	**[LL19] Jeong-Yup Lee and Dong-il Lee**, *Gröbner–Shirshov bases for non-crystallographic Coxeter groups*, *Acta Crystallographica A* 75 (2019), 584–592, [arXiv:1809.08419](https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.08419), [DOI:10.1107/S2053273319002092](https://doi.org/10.1107/S2053273319002092). **Opened abstract and PDF.** Gives group-algebra GS bases and standard monomials for H2, H3, H4; degree-lex with `s1<s2<s3<s4` as appropriate. These are finite **Coxeter** groups, not the corresponding Artin groups.
    51	
    52	**[PW26] Xiaowei Pang and Jun Wang**, *Gröbner–Shirshov bases for the Coxeter groups of the types G2, F4, E6, E7 and E8*, [arXiv:2002.07369](https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.07369), first posted 2020; **local PDF is v2, 15 March 2026**. **Opened abstract and PDF.** Theorems 3.2/3.3/3.4 give E6/E7/E8; Appendix A gives G2/F4. Uses degree-lex and the rearranged/reflection-generator labeling of Definition 3.1; for E_n the letters are `x_{7-n},...,x6`, with the branch at `x3`. The paper's generator relabeling is material to its smaller basis; do not substitute another fixed diagram ordering without checking. The defining relations explicitly include squares equal to 1. The abstract/metadata fetched by the arXiv reader differs slightly from v2's printed abstract; v2's title page has **both Pang and Wang**, not Wang alone. No journal publication was established in this search. The authors say the finite irreducible list is complete; this survey has not independently checked their computations or historical priority. In particular [US24] cites a 2007 E8 master's thesis, so “E8 first solved in 2026” would be unwarranted.
    53	
    54	**[US24] Uğur Ustaoğlu**, *Gröbner–Shirshov bases and normal forms for the infinite Coxeter groups of types B-tilde_n and D-tilde_n*, *Journal of Universal Mathematics* 7, special memorial issue (2024), 86–101, [DOI:10.33773/jum.1562793](https://doi.org/10.33773/jum.1562793). **Opened publisher abstract and PDF.** Standard Coxeter alphabets `s0,...,sn`, degree-lex; Lemma 2.2 and the corresponding D section give basis families and normal forms. These are affine Coxeter, not affine Artin, results.
    55	
    56	**[BCS07] L. A. Bokut, V. V. Chaynikov and K. P. Shum**, *Markov and Artin normal form theorem for braid groups*, *Communications in Algebra* 35 (2007), 2105–2115, [arXiv:0806.1124](https://arxiv.org/abs/0806.1124), [DOI:10.1080/00927870701302115](https://doi.org/10.1080/00927870701302115). **Opened PDF.** Artin–Markov relations form the GS basis in Artin–Burau generators for ordinary braids, using inverse-tower order, with deg-inverse-lex inside the stated blocks. The 2014 survey, §3.1.1, also notes a subsequent full composition treatment by Chen–Mo (2009); the original paper's conclusion is not a type-B result.
    57	
    58	**[BG08] L. A. Bokut**, *Gröbner–Shirshov basis for the braid group in the Artin–Garside generators*, *Journal of Symbolic Computation* 43 (2008), 397–405, [arXiv:0806.1125](https://arxiv.org/abs/0806.1125), [DOI:10.1016/j.jsc.2007.02.003](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jsc.2007.02.003). **Opened PDF.** Theorem 1.1 is an explicit family of GS relations for ordinary `B_{n+1}` on `a1,...,an,Delta,Delta^{-1}`. Word order is degree-lex with `Delta^{-1}<Delta<a1<...<an`. Separate inverse letters `a_i^{-1}` are unnecessary because of complements in Delta. Irreducibles give Garside normal forms. The positive-word parameters make the displayed family generally infinite.
    59	
    60	**[BKL09] L. A. Bokut**, *Gröbner–Shirshov basis for the braid group in the Birman–Ko–Lee generators*, *Journal of Algebra* 321 (2009), 361–376, [arXiv:0806.1123](https://arxiv.org/abs/0806.1123), [DOI:10.1016/j.jalgebra.2008.10.007](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jalgebra.2008.10.007). **Opened PDF.** The preprint title says “Birman–Ko–Lee–Garside generators”, accurately indicating the extra `delta^{±1}`. Gives a GS basis for ordinary braids; degree-lex with `delta^{-1}<delta<a_ts<a_rq` iff `(t,s)<(r,q)` lexicographically. Not an all-finite-type theorem.
    61	
    62	**[BFKS03] L. A. Bokut, Y. Fong, W.-F. Ke and L.-S. Shiao**, *Gröbner–Shirshov bases for the braid semigroup*, in *Advances in Algebra* (2003), 60–72, [arXiv:0806.1118](https://arxiv.org/abs/0806.1118), [DOI:10.1142/9789812705808_0005](https://doi.org/10.1142/9789812705808_0005). **Opened PDF.** Gives the positive type-A braid-monoid basis in standard Artin generators and degree-lex; general Artin-monoid formulas are conjectural in its introduction. Do not date the original theorem to 2008 merely because of the later arXiv deposit.
    63	
    64	**[BV06] Leonid Bokut and Andrei Vesnin**, *Gröbner–Shirshov bases for some braid groups*, *Journal of Symbolic Computation* 41 (2006), 357–371, [DOI:10.1016/j.jsc.2005.09.009](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jsc.2005.09.009), [author PDF](https://math.nsc.ru/~vesnin/papers/bokut-vesnin2006.pdf). **Opened PDF**, after ScienceDirect returned 403. Propositions 3.1 and 3.2 cover **all two-generator Artin groups** `A(m)`, not only `A(3)=B3`. For `m=2n`, use `t=x`, `y_i=t^i y t^{-i}` (`0<=i<n`) and their inverses, a free-by-cyclic presentation, and tower order. For `m=2n+1`, use `t=x`, conjugates `z_i=t^i(yx^{-1})t^{-i}` (`0<=i<2n`) and inverses. For B3/B4, Gorin–Lin generators/tower order give bases and normal forms (Theorem 5.1 for B4). This settles the rank-two **changed-alphabet** problem, not the book's named Burau/Garside-alphabet version.
    65	
    66	**[CZ13] Yuqun Chen and Chanyan Zhong**, *Gröbner–Shirshov bases for braid groups in Adyan–Thurston generators*, *Algebra Colloquium* 20(2) (2013), 309–318, [arXiv:0909.3639](https://arxiv.org/abs/0909.3639), [DOI:10.1142/S1005386713000278](https://doi.org/10.1142/S1005386713000278). **Opened abstract, publisher DOI abstract and PDF.** Type A is treated in §2; **type B** is treated in §3. Alphabet: `r(alpha)` for every nonidentity element of the appropriate finite Coxeter group, plus `Delta^{-1}` for the group. Letters satisfy `r(alpha)<r(beta)` if Coxeter length of alpha is *larger*, with lex tie-break; use degree-lex on words. Theorems 3.3 and 3.6 give the positive-monoid and group bases; Corollary 3.7 gives `Delta^k r(alpha1)...r(alphas)`. This is an important correction to reading the 2014 survey's “except braid groups/type A” sentence literally as exhaustive.
    67	
    68	**[AU07] Marc Autord**, *Comparing Gröbner bases and word reversing*, arXiv preprint (2007), [arXiv:0712.0525](https://arxiv.org/abs/0712.0525). **Opened abstract and PDF**, especially §3.1. Shows the two completion procedures can differ radically. Example 3.3 supplies the reduced positive B3 basis `{bab=aba} ∪ {ba^n ba=aba^2 b^{n-1}: n>=2}`, degree-lex `b>a`. Proposition 3.4 treats noncommuting two-generator Artin presentations and shows infinite degree-lex completion despite finite reversing-completeness; its even-parameter proof explicitly gives `{(ba)^n=(ab)^n} ∪ {ba^p(ab)^n=(ab)^n ba^p: p>=1}` (use `n>=2` for the noncommuting case). Thus positive G2/dihedral completion is not wholly unstudied. Proposition 3.5 proves infinitude of degree-lex completion for ordinary positive braid monoids with at least three strands. These are **positive monoid**, not Delta/inverse group, results; no claim about every possible monomial order is made here.
    69	
    70	**[IMAN24] Zaffar Iqbal, Muhammad Mobeen Munir, Maleeha Ayub and Abdul Rauf Nizami**, *Complete presentation and Hilbert series of the mixed braid monoid MB_{1,3}*, *Revista de la Unión Matemática Argentina* 67(2) (2024), 503–516, [DOI:10.33044/revuma.3479](https://doi.org/10.33044/revuma.3479), [publisher PDF](https://inmabb.criba.edu.ar/revuma/pdf/v67n2/v67n2a06.pdf). **Opened PDF.** Page 505 explicitly identifies this monoid with the **Artin monoid of type B3**. Theorem 2.3 gives standard-positive-generator completion families for `z3z2z3=z2z3z2`, `z2z1z2z1=z1z2z1z2`, `z3z1=z1z3`, with length-lex `z1<z2<z3`. There are eleven parameterized additional families, not eleven individual finite rules. No inverse/Delta group completion is supplied. This 2024 paper is a genuine non-A positive-monoid result missed by a search limited to titles containing “Artin” or “Gröbner–Shirshov”. It was found through Hilbert-series/citation trails. The basis calculation has not been independently audited here.
