    76	| **[HM99] Susan M. Hermiller and John Meier**, *Artin groups, rewriting systems and three-manifolds*, *Journal of Pure and Applied Algebra* 136 (1999), 141–156; [DOI:10.1016/S0022-4049(97)00171-0](https://doi.org/10.1016/S0022-4049(97)00171-0), [author PDF](https://www.math.unl.edu/~shermiller2/webppr/artin.pdf). **Opened PDF.** Included although before the main search window because essential to interpreting the question. | Theorem 1: every finite-type Artin **group** has a geodesic finite complete rewriting system. §3 uses `S={ [w]: w in W\{1} }`, i.e. every nonidentity positive simple. First alphabet `S ∪ {Delta^{-1}}`; second alphabet `S ∪ S^{-1}`. Theorem 2 additionally treats groups whose defining graph is a tree. | §3.1 orients a **right-greedy** system by shortlex: positive letters are ordered by their underlying Coxeter length, and the inverse Delta letter is largest. §3.2 gives the signed-simple system with the corresponding positive/negative length ordering. These are explicit finite complete systems, not merely existence of a normal form. Neither alphabet is `{sigma_i,Delta^{±1}}`. The printed overbars denote inverse letters and are lost in plain-text extraction. |
    77	| **[GGM15] Stéphane Gaussent, Yves Guiraud and Philippe Malbos**, *Coherent presentations of Artin monoids*, *Compositio Mathematica* 151 (2015), 957–998; [arXiv:1203.5358](https://arxiv.org/abs/1203.5358). **Opened abstract and PDF.** | Proposition 3.2.1 gives a coherent **convergent** presentation of the Artin **monoid** on `W\{1}`: length-additive multiplication rules `u|v -> uv` and completion/sliding rules `u|vw -> uv|w` with the exact length conditions in §3.1. The alphabet/rule set is finite for finite W and generally infinite otherwise. Theorem 4.1.1 gives a coherent presentation on **standard atoms**, with one Tits–Zamolodchikov 3-cell for each finite rank-three parabolic. | §3.2's termination comparison first uses number of simple letters and then their Coxeter lengths from the right. The standard-atom result is **coherence**, not a claim that the oriented Artin relations are confluent. Group inverses are not part of these monoid theorems. |
    78	| **[DDH15] Patrick Dehornoy, Matthew Dyer and Christophe Hohlweg**, *Garside families in Artin–Tits monoids and low elements in Coxeter groups*, *Comptes Rendus Mathématique* 353 (2015), 403–408; [arXiv:1411.7498](https://arxiv.org/abs/1411.7498). **Opened abstract and PDF.** | Theorem 1.1: every finitely generated Artin–Tits **monoid** has a finite Garside family. Theorem 1.2 concerns low Coxeter elements, including finiteness and closure under suffix/join. | The arXiv abstract says “group”, but the actual theorem says **monoid**; use the theorem. This provides the finite alphabet used by [DG16], not a group presentation on standard atoms. |
    79	| **[DG16] Patrick Dehornoy and Yves Guiraud**, *Quadratic normalization in monoids*, *International Journal of Algebra and Computation* 26(5) (2016), 935–972; [arXiv:1504.02717](https://arxiv.org/abs/1504.02717), [DOI:10.1142/S0218196716500399](https://doi.org/10.1142/S0218196716500399). **Opened abstract and PDF.** | Proposition 6.3.3: for a left-cancellative monoid with no nontrivial units and Garside family S, rules normalize each **pair of S-letters** to its minimal-length S-normal decomposition; the resulting system on `S\{1}` is convergent. Corollary 6.3.4: every finitely generated Artin–Tits monoid admits a **finite quadratic convergent presentation**. In spherical type S can be all simples/positive W-lifts. | Theorems B/C and Proposition 5.2.1 establish termination via class-(4,3) normalization/domino arguments; this is not a theorem about standard-atom degree-lex. The bound in Theorem B is `2^p-p-1`, not `2p-p-1` (the PDF-to-text superscript is lost). “Quadratic” allows a length-two left side and a shorter normal output after deleting the unit letter. |
    80	| **[BS07] Patrick Bahls and Tyler Smith**, *Rewriting systems and orderings on Artin monoids*, *International Journal of Algebra and Computation* 17(1) (2007), 61–75; [DOI:10.1142/S0218196707003482](https://doi.org/10.1142/S0218196707003482). **Opened publisher-supplied abstract via DOI/Crossref; full text not opened.** | Constructs a complete rewriting system for every **large-type Artin monoid**, derived from Burckel's well-ordering, and studies associated well-orderability. | Do not infer finiteness, exact alphabet, or the requested Delta-order completion from the abstract. “Large type” does not include all spherical types: commuting pairs matter. |
