   151	- **E. G. Karpuz, F. Ateş, N. Urlu, A. S. Çevik and I. N. Cangul**, *A note on the Gröbner–Shirshov bases over ad-hoc extensions of groups*, *Filomat* (2016), [DOI:10.2298/FIL1604037K](https://doi.org/10.2298/FIL1604037K). **Opened DOI abstract only.** Knit products of cyclic groups and iterated semidirect products of free groups; no specified non-A Artin alphabet in the abstract.
   152	- **E. G. Karpuz, F. Ateş and A. S. Çevik**, *Gröbner–Shirshov bases of some Weyl groups*, *Rocky Mountain Journal of Mathematics* 45 (2015), 1165–1175, [DOI:10.1216/RMJ-2015-45-4-1165](https://doi.org/10.1216/RMJ-2015-45-4-1165). **Opened DOI abstract only.** Extended/elliptic Weyl groups with toroidal-type generators, not spherical Artin groups.
   153	- *Gröbner–Shirshov bases of some monoids*, DOI `10.1016/j.disc.2011.03.008`, and *Gröbner–Shirshov bases of some semigroup constructions*, DOI `10.1142/S100538671500005X`, remained **[UNVERIFIED] for content** (metadata/no abstract or 403). They are not used as proof of a null result.
   154	
   155	**Citation-chain conclusion:** no opened citing paper gives the requested non-A **group** GS basis in the small alphabet `{sigma_i,Delta^{±1}}`, and none supplies the missing uniform Artin–Burau result. This is a bounded bibliographic observation. API coverage is incomplete, counts are not total worldwide citation counts, and failure to index a paper is not evidence of nonexistence.
   156	
   157	### D3. Standard-generator lex/shortlex languages: what is actually known here
   158	
   159	| Source and opened flag | Exact language/alphabet | What it does not establish |
   160	|---|---|---|
   161	| **[HR12] Derek F. Holt and Sarah Rees**, *Artin groups of large type are shortlex automatic with regular geodesics*, *Proceedings of the London Mathematical Society* 104 (2012), 486–512; [arXiv:1003.6007](https://arxiv.org/abs/1003.6007), [DOI:10.1112/plms/pdr035](https://doi.org/10.1112/plms/pdr035). **Opened abstract and PDF**, §§1–2. | Standard signed alphabet `X ∪ X^{-1}`, **any ordering**. Large type: every finite off-diagonal Artin parameter is at least 3; no commuting pair. Shortlex automaticity and FFTP/regularity of all geodesics. The paper separately proves the two-generator versions for every `m>=2` (Theorems 2.4/2.6), hence covers G2/dihedral directly. | It does not cover all spherical types: A3, B3 and higher familiar types contain a commuting pair. Shortlex automaticity is not itself a finite GS-rule list. |
   162	| **Fuchiwaki–Fujii–Saito–Tsuchioka**, *Geodesic automata and growth functions for Artin monoids of finite type*, RIMS preprint 1646 (2008), [primary PDF](https://www.kurims.kyoto-u.ac.jp/preprint/file/RIMS1646.pdf). **Opened PDF**, abstract and §§4–5. | Constructs a finite-state **unique geodesic cross-section on the standard positive atoms for every finite-type Artin monoid**. It replaces each simple letter in a Charney language by a chosen atom-word lift `iota`; §5.2 makes the dependence on this lift explicit. Then determinizes/minimizes the acceptor. | The theorem is not that these words are globally **lexicographically least** among all atom-words. “Geodesic” alone is weak for a homogeneous positive monoid: all positive representatives of an element have the same atom length. This does not prove a standard-atom shortlex group language or an atom-plus-Delta GS basis. |
   163	| **Ramón Flores and Juan González-Meneses**, *On lexicographic representatives in braid monoids*, *Journal of Algebraic Combinatorics* 52 (2020), 561–597; [arXiv:1808.02755](https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.02755). **Opened abstract and PDF**, introduction and §2. | The minimal/maximal lexicographic representatives of the **positive type-A braid monoid** on standard atoms are regular. Describes the minimal DFA, following Gebhardt–González-Meneses, with detailed state counts. Positive relations preserve length, so minimal lex is also shortlex in this monoid setting. | Not a theorem for all finite types, not a theorem for signed group words, and not a theorem after adding Delta. |
   164	| **Derek F. Holt and Sarah Rees**, *Artin groups of type (2,3,n)*, arXiv preprint (2023), [arXiv:2310.14632](https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.14632). **Opened abstract and PDF**, Theorem 1.1. | For the rank-three presentation `aba=bab`, `ac=ca`, `[bc]_n=[cb]_n`, `n>=5`, length-preserving rules plus free cancellation reduce signed standard-generator words to **a geodesic representative** in quadratic time. This includes the spherical H3 case `n=5`. | Does not assert a unique shortlex normal form, a regular shortlex language, or a confluent GS presentation. Do not infer those stronger results from a geodesic word-problem algorithm. |
   165	| **Sarah Rees**, *The development of the theory of automatic groups*, 2022 survey, [arXiv:2205.14911](https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.14911). **Opened PDF**, §5.1. | Explicitly says Charney's spherical-type biautomatic structures are geodesic over **Garside/simple-element generators, not the standard generators**. Also summarizes standard-generator shortlex for large/sufficiently large type. | Ordinary automaticity/biautomaticity is invariant under change of finite generators, but **being the shortlex language for a particular alphabet/order is not supplied by that invariance**. |
   166	
   167	Thus there are strong positive standard-alphabet results, especially rank two, type-A positive monoids, and standard-atom *cross-sections* for all spherical positive monoids. **No opened source in this search establishes a uniform regular lex/shortlex language for every spherical Artin group over the standard signed generators, or for every non-A group over `{sigma_i,Delta^{±1}}`.** Likewise the all-finite-type monoid cross-section source must not be advertised as an all-finite-type lex-minimal theorem. These are search-scoped statements, not claims that these language questions are proven open.
